{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794589123,"nonce":"M3X42duYQpWeMCGqi0n/Hv3458w=","message":"Record fields now support #[hxn(default)] for Rust Default::default() and #[hxn(default = \"path\")] for a public zero-argument function relative to the library root. Haxe may omit the field or set it to null to request the default. Explicit values, including zero, false and empty collections, are serialized as present. The adapter materializes omitted fields lazily, without mutating the Haxe input; Rust always returns present values. Option fields retain omission as None and reject this annotation, including through a Box. Tagged-enum fields remain required.\n\nThe manifest records default: true or the custom function path. Each defaulted field adds a presence byte, so generated adapters and Haxe bindings must be regenerated together when an annotation changes. Unannotated fields keep their encoding. No ABI implementation, layout, dependency or runtime export changed; hxn-abi has only a wire-format documentation update and hxn-future is unchanged.\n\nThe parser checks malformed, duplicate and unsupported notes, invalid function paths, nullable defaults and enum-field defaults. Generator tests cover Rust/Haxe codecs, CPPIA classes and manifest metadata. The fixture checks trait and custom defaults, lazy evaluation, present values on a second crossing, input isolation, explicit overrides, null resets, nested lists, futures, signals, callbacks, numeric ranges and required-null rejection. Separate Haxe probes reject missing required fields and incorrect scalar/nested types. The pantry example omits unit and Rust fills Item::default_unit; its output stays the same. Both manifests and the design/convention documents are current.\n\nValidation, with platform tests under timeouts:\n- macOS arm64: workspace tests, strict Clippy, formatting and diff checks pass. All nine counter modes pass. CLI and examples pass on Ash hybrid/JIT/interpreter, hxcpp, CPPIA, Ash wasm and Emscripten. C imports pass in every supported mode, including native SQLite where supported. Both native counter fixtures pass three repetitions per Ash mode; generated hxcpp and CPPIA pass three times, and generated wasm/Emscripten pass twice after their initial runs.\n- Linux x86-64 on the NUC: workspace tests, strict Clippy, stock HashLink fixtures, generated Ash/hxcpp/CPPIA fixtures, and all native CLI/example/import modes pass. Generated stock HashLink and both fixtures on Ash hybrid/JIT/interpreter pass three repetitions each. All 179 tracked source files match local SHA-256 hashes; task-local Haxe/hxcpp configuration was used.\n- Mobile: every example passes twice each on the iPhone 15 iOS 17.5 simulator (iphonesim) and Pixel 9 Pro Android emulator through ADB. Both devices were shut down afterwards.\n- Miri: hxn-abi passes all 35 tests. hxn-future reports 30 passes and four failures; every failing test also reproduces individually on a clean archive of committed HEAD 16da3fa. These pre-existing scheduler timing assumptions are tracked separately in e841a1587924121da45e63f9f3d3791696be8b23274789deec2ea3ae2d20403d. No Miri undefined-behaviour report appeared.\n\nNot run: Windows (awaiting push/CI), Linux wasm/Emscripten (both ran on macOS), or physical mobile hardware. Optional SQLite is skipped on the NUC because sqlite3.h is absent, and by the Emscripten import script.\n\nThe record-default changes are uncommitted and not pushed. This broader generator issue remains open for its other gaps.","files":null}]}